# -*- mode:org; coding:utf-8 -*-
#+TITLE: windows-build-locked-executable — a running old exe blocks every later build on a Windows runner
#+STATUS: DONE
#+PRIORITY: P1
#+TYPE: bug
#+BRANCH: windows-locked-exe-build

* Symptom

From 2026-10-03 20:09Z every job on the Windows CAD runner PF5S26B that
builds alfe (probe:suite:bricscad:windows, verify:bricscad-hidden-ui:windows)
failed in seconds:

: ...\autolisp-front-end\tools\alfe\bin\alfe-sbcl.exe: Permission denied
: Unhandled SB-IMPL::SAVE-ERROR ... Could not save core.

Every one of them had passed up to 19:58Z.

* Cause

At 19:55-19:58Z the hung probe:suite:autocad:windows job and its
probe:suite:bricscad:windows sibling were cancelled. Cancelling a job on the
Windows runner does not kill the processes it started, so an alfe-sbcl.exe
from that job kept running. Windows refuses to overwrite or delete a running
executable, and the builds share one builds directory, so SAVE-LISP-AND-DIE
could no longer write bin/alfe-sbcl.exe.

* Fix (clautolisp 2.2.166)

The shared generator (autolisp-reader/tools/read-autolisp/generate.lisp),
which builds read-autolisp, clautolisp and alfe, now calls
%MOVE-ASIDE-OLD-EXECUTABLE before saving. It deletes the old executable;
when Windows keeps that executable locked, it renames it to
<exe>.old-<universal-time> instead, which Windows allows for a running
image. Leftovers from earlier builds are deleted once they are free.

The stale process still holds the file and BricsCAD may be stuck with it:
killing stray alfe-sbcl.exe / bricscad processes on PF5S26B is still worth
doing by hand.
